    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "<|channel>thought\n<channel|>That's a pretty bold stance. You're saying targeted programs are \"inherently flawed,\" but many economists argue that they are far more efficient because they direct resources exactly where they are needed most, rather than giving money to people who don't need it. How do you account for the massive fiscal cost and potential inflation risks that come with a UBI?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
